# RUNBOOK — Python API behind nginx (Compose)

Small JSON API stack: `proxy` (nginx, unprivileged) → `api` (gunicorn/Flask) →
`db` (Postgres) + `cache` (Redis). Compose is used as a single-host process
supervisor, not as an orchestrator.

Service names on the Docker networks (use these, never container IDs):

| Service | Internal DNS | Port | Network   | Published to host |
|---------|--------------|------|-----------|-------------------|
| proxy   | `proxy`      | 8080 | `edge`    | `8080`            |
| api     | `api`        | 8000 | `edge`, `internal` | no     |
| db      | `db`         | 5432 | `internal`| no                |
| cache   | `cache`      | 6379 | `internal`| no                |

The `internal` network has no outbound routing. Only the proxy is published.

Application contract expected by this stack:

- Module `app:app` (WSGI callable) in the image workdir `/app`.
- `GET /healthz` returns 2xx when the process can serve traffic (used by the
  api container healthcheck). Keep it cheap: process liveness plus a trivial
  dependency ping at most.
- `GET /nginx-health` is answered by nginx itself and never touches the API.

---

